---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Data">
  <main>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Why the current health-data system fails</h2>
    <p class="desc">The United States has many capable electronic record
      systems, but not a dependable system-level record. Data exchange varies
      by vendor, payer, facility, and setting. A person can therefore have
      several incomplete versions of the same history while clinicians still
      lack the medication, result, discharge, or referral information needed
      for the next decision.</p>
    <div class="data-fix-head" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>Current system problem</span>
      <span>leads to</span>
      <span>How the system fixes it</span>
    </div>
    <div id="data-fixes" class="data-fix-grid"
      aria-label="Current health-data failures and the system controls that correct them"></div>
    <p class="note">This is treated as a safety and continuity
      problem, not merely an IT inconvenience. The status-quo comparison records
      incomplete interoperability as a cause of duplicate testing, missing
      medication and history, failed follow-up, and migration risk. It also
      treats concentrated cyber and payment-rail failures as a separate
      national continuity hazard.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>One national system, many authoritative nodes</h2>
    <p class="desc">The proposed National Health Assurance Information Mesh is
      a single governed data system, but deliberately not one giant database.
      Hospitals, laboratories, pharmacies, clinicians, regional systems, and
      public agencies may retain the records they are responsible for. The
      mesh supplies the common identity, locator, vocabulary, API, access,
      audit, correction, and cybersecurity rules that make those records work
      as one longitudinal system.</p>
    <div class="data-thesis">
      <div>
        <span class="data-kicker">One coherent public rail</span>
        <strong>Shared meaning, identity, rights, and accountability</strong>
      </div>
      <div class="data-thesis-divider" aria-hidden="true">≠</div>
      <div>
        <span class="data-kicker">Not one uncontrolled store</span>
        <strong>Source custody stays distributed and attributable</strong>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="data-lifecycle" aria-label="How a record moves through the information mesh">
      <div class="data-life-step">
        <span>1</span><b>Resolve</b>
        <small>Match the patient, provider, facility, and acting role.</small>
      </div>
      <div class="data-life-step">
        <span>2</span><b>Locate</b>
        <small>Find the governed source records needed for this purpose.</small>
      </div>
      <div class="data-life-step">
        <span>3</span><b>Exchange</b>
        <small>Move the minimum necessary fields through a certified API.</small>
      </div>
      <div class="data-life-step">
        <span>4</span><b>Use and write</b>
        <small>Read the longitudinal view and record the new event once.</small>
      </div>
      <div class="data-life-step">
        <span>5</span><b>Audit and reconcile</b>
        <small>Log access, close follow-up, correct errors, and sync after outages.</small>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>What the system stores</h2>
    <p class="desc">The plan defines logical records rather than a
      vendor-specific database. Every record carries its authority, steward,
      schema version, event and effective times, provenance, access class,
      consent or purpose, correction chain, quality flags, and retention
      state. That makes a clinical fact, payment, appeal, or published metric
      reproducible instead of silently overwritable.</p>
    <div id="data-planes" class="data-plane-grid"
      aria-label="Six logical data planes in the national information mesh"></div>
    <div class="tbl-scroll data-store-wrap">
      <table class="data data-store-table" id="data-store-table">
        <caption class="sr-only">Major information-mesh datasets and examples</caption>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card data-roadmap-card">
    <h2>How the data system comes online</h2>
    <p class="desc">The information mesh is not switched on nationally in
      one step. Select a phase to see the data capabilities built in that
      period and the performance records that should govern progression. Values
      marked <b>Specified</b> are controlled milestones or source targets;
      values marked <b>Derived</b> are proposed interim thresholds with a
      documented rationale.</p>
    <div class="data-target-legend" aria-label="Phase target status legend">
      <span><i class="data-basis framework">Specified</i> controlled value</span>
      <span><i class="data-basis derived">Derived</i> proposed interim value</span>
    </div>
    <div id="data-phase-timeline" class="rollout-timeline data-phase-timeline"
      aria-label="Interactive data-system rollout timeline"></div>
    <div id="data-phase-detail" class="rollout-detail data-phase-detail"
      aria-live="polite"></div>
    <p class="note">Early-phase derived targets apply to the stated test,
      pilot, participating-provider, or Wave I cohort. They are not national
      claims. <a id="data-methodology-link"
        href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/data_phase_target_methodology.md#p0"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">See every derivation and justification</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>How healthcare providers interact with it</h2>
    <p class="desc">Providers do not abandon every local workflow or type into
      a separate federal portal. A certified local EHR, hospital system,
      pharmacy terminal, laboratory system, or diagnostic-treatment unit
      workstation connects through the national gateway. The shared layer
      supplies the current permitted view and receives the new care event,
      while the creating source remains attributable.</p>
    <div class="provider-flow" aria-label="Provider workflow with the national information mesh">
      <div class="provider-step">
        <span>01</span><b>Authenticate</b>
        <p>Verify clinician identity, credentials, facility, role, and declared purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="provider-step">
        <span>02</span><b>Open the permitted view</b>
        <p>Locate medications, allergies, results, care plans, prior encounters, and active referrals.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="provider-step">
        <span>03</span><b>Deliver and record care</b>
        <p>Write the encounter, order, result, treatment, medication, disposition, and accountable owner.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="provider-step">
        <span>04</span><b>Route and close the loop</b>
        <p>Send structured referrals, handoffs, urgency, results, returned plans, and follow-up closure.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="provider-step">
        <span>05</span><b>Correct or recover</b>
        <p>Supersede errors without erasing history; queue bounded offline events and reconcile on recovery.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="data-provider-notes">
      <div>
        <b>Diagnostic-treatment units</b>
        <p>Every Type A-D encounter writes request, acuity, tests, treatment,
          escalation, and follow-up state into the shared record by design.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Patients</b>
        <p>The Patient Access and Consent Portal provides the longitudinal
          view, usable export, correction request, segmentation controls, and
          a visible accounting of who accessed which data and why.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Emergency and downtime care</b>
        <p>Emergency routing, medication access, minimal record lookup,
          claims intake, and payment keep bounded offline or manual paths;
          every event is reconciled after service returns.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Data transfer across the national information mesh</h2>
    <p class="desc">The center is the shared coordination layer. The care
      network on the left creates and consumes person-level clinical events.
      Public operating, rights, payment, oversight, and research institutions
      on the right exchange only the fields their lawful function requires.
      Each connection is attributable, purpose-bound, and auditable.</p>
    <div class="data-map-legend" aria-label="Data transfer legend">
      <span><i class="legend-line bidir" aria-hidden="true">↔</i> governed read and write</span>
      <span><i class="legend-line source" aria-hidden="true"></i> authoritative source remains identified</span>
      <span><i class="legend-line segment" aria-hidden="true"></i> segmented or aggregate-only where required</span>
    </div>
    <div id="data-transfer-map" class="data-transfer-map"
      aria-label="Map of data exchange between the national information mesh and interacting facilities and institutions"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Cyber safety is part of care delivery</h2>
    <p class="desc">Because the information mesh becomes critical national
      infrastructure, privacy alone is not enough. Confidentiality, integrity,
      availability, safe degraded operation, and accountable recovery are all
      clinical requirements. A system that is online but serving stale,
      incomplete, or corrupted data is treated as degraded.</p>
    <div id="cyber-controls" class="cyber-control-grid"
      aria-label="Required cybersecurity and continuity controls"></div>
    <div class="tiles cyber-targets" aria-label="Cybersecurity performance targets">
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Critical-system uptime</div>
        <div class="value">≥99.97%</div>
        <div class="range">with unsafe or stale service classified as degraded</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Downtime continuity</div>
        <div class="value">≥98%</div>
        <div class="range">of essential-care and payment drills passed</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Critical remediation</div>
        <div class="value">≥99%</div>
        <div class="range">of critical vulnerabilities fixed within 15 days</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Sensitive-access audits</div>
        <div class="value">≥99%</div>
        <div class="range">of required cases completed and reviewable</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="rollout-source">Derived phase targets and their assumptions are
    documented in the
    <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/data_phase_target_methodology.md"
      target="_blank" rel="noopener">data phase-target methodology</a>.</p>

    <ChapterNav />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/data-client.ts';
</script>
